================================================================================
2. Close read of the one check this item consists of
================================================================================

A workflow has no unit test. Its check is the compare job, so that is the test
I read closely, line by line: what is on the left, what is on the right, and
where each side comes from.

  left  = the contents of linux-digests/kernel.sha256, read by
          LINUX_KERNEL=$(cat linux-digests/kernel.sha256)
  right = the contents of windows-digests/kernel.sha256, read by
          WIN_KERNEL=$(cat windows-digests/kernel.sha256)
  test  = [ "$LINUX_KERNEL" != "$WIN_KERNEL" ] -> FAIL=1 -> exit 1
  and the same three lines again for image.sha256 against nova.hdd.

Where each side comes from, traced in the file:

  build-linux  (runs-on: ubuntu-latest)
     NOVA_TEST=0 bash scripts/build-disk.sh release
     sha256sum kernel/target/x86_64-unknown-none/release/nucleus | awk '{print $1}' > build-digests/kernel.sha256
     sha256sum build/nova.hdd                                      | awk '{print $1}' > build-digests/image.sha256
     actions/upload-artifact digests-linux

  build-windows (runs-on: windows-latest, shell: bash on every step)
     the same four commands, byte for byte, with the same two paths.

  compare       (runs-on: ubuntu-latest, needs: [build-linux, build-windows])
     downloads both artifact sets and compares them as above.

This is worth stating plainly, because it is exactly the trap AGENTS.md rule 1
describes: both sides of the assertion are produced by the same command text,
in the same file, written by the same author. A shared misconception - hashing
the wrong file, or hashing nothing - is invisible to the comparison. What the
comparison *can* detect is a difference between two machines, and that is all
C5 asks for (docs/reviews/c5-freebuff.log finding F1 was "same-host only").
The independence C5 needs is the two runners, not two authors. I checked the
runners were real and distinct in section 3.

The workflow's own plant (the one 0353f6e claims) is:

  if: ${{ inputs.plant == true || contains(github.ref, 'plant/') }}
  printf '\nconst _REPRO_PLANT: &str = "%s";\n' "$(hostname)-$(date +%s%N)" >> kernel/src/main.rs
  sed -i 's/sprintln!("NOVA_BOOT_OK");/sprintln!("NOVA_BOOT_OK {}", _REPRO_PLANT);/' kernel/src/main.rs

so the Linux build gets a per-run hostname and nanosecond timestamp in the
kernel source and the Windows build does not.

