7. F7, F2.11's unsure item: the two failed repro runs on plant branches
================================================================================

F2 reported: "both of my plant branches' repro runs failed cross-host hash
comparison (42265842709, 42265860068) while a3dd44f's repro run passed minutes
earlier, and a one-constant change cannot explain host divergence."

Both runs are on branches whose names start with plant/:

$ gh run view 42265842709 --json headBranch,headSha --jq '.headBranch'
plant/f2.11-880
$ gh run view 42265860068 --json headBranch,headSha --jq '.headBranch'
plant/f2.11-wait

That is the whole explanation. The workflow turns its own hostname/timestamp
plant on for any ref containing "plant/":

$ grep -E "_REPRO_PLANT: &str|RESULT:" f7a.log | sed 's/.*Z //'
+const _REPRO_PLANT: &str = "runnervm7aw5e-1793987664622000522";
RESULT: C5 cross-host reproducibility GATE FAILED
$ grep -E "_REPRO_PLANT: &str|RESULT:" f7b.log | sed 's/.*Z //'
+const _REPRO_PLANT: &str = "runnervm7aw5e-1793987670747183809";
RESULT: C5 cross-host reproducibility GATE FAILED

Both branches change one line of kernel/src/hda.rs (440 -> 880 Hz):

$ git diff --stat origin/main...origin/plant/f2.11-880
 kernel/src/hda.rs | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)
$ git diff --stat origin/main...origin/plant/f2.11-wait
 kernel/src/hda.rs | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

So the Windows hash in those runs legitimately differs from main's (the branch
changed the kernel), and the Linux hash additionally differs from the Windows
one because the workflow appended a hostname and timestamp to main.rs on Linux
by itself. The gate failed for a real difference - just not the branch's own.
It is not a flake, and a3dd44f's repro run passing minutes earlier is consistent:
a3dd44f is not a plant branch, so no hostname was planted there.

Recorded as finding N2, because the consequence is operational: every plant
branch in this repository now produces a red repro run that says nothing about
that branch's reproducibility, and a reviewer reading the Actions list sees a
failure that was manufactured by the branch name.

================================================================================
8. Findings
================================================================================

N1 (non-blocking, owner A1, .github/workflows/repro.yml). The two build jobs run
identical digest steps under different shells: ubuntu's implicit "bash -e {0}"
does not fail a pipeline whose last command succeeded, windows' "shell: bash"
adds -o pipefail and does. My P2 shows the Ubuntu job uploading an empty
kernel.sha256 and staying green while Windows failed. The gate is not
misleading in that exact case, because an empty digest on one side makes the
comparison print DIFFER and exit 1 - but only if the other side produced a
digest and the compare job gets to run at all. Suggested fix, small: start each
digest step with "set -euo pipefail" and assert the result is 64 hex characters
before writing it, for example
  H=$(sha256sum FILE | awk '{print $1}'); [ ${#H} -eq 64 ] || exit 1; echo "$H" > ...
The same one-line guard in the compare job would make "the gate measured
nothing" a red run instead of a green one.

N2 (non-blocking, owner A1 and the lead, .github/workflows/repro.yml). The
contains(github.ref, 'plant/') condition makes every plant branch fail repro by
construction. It answered F2.11's F7 question, and it is doing what its commit
message says it does, but it should be either reported in the run name (so the
Actions list distinguishes "planted on purpose" from "not reproducible") or
triggered only by workflow_dispatch.

N3 (design note, not a defect I can plant fairly). The compared values are two
text files produced inside the build jobs and uploaded as artifacts; nothing
binds them to the actual binaries, and the compare job never sees the ELF or the
image. Anyone editing the digest step to hash a wrong-but-existing file would
hollow the gate out on both hosts symmetrically, and no assertion in the
workflow would notice. Fixing it means uploading the two artifacts and letting
the compare job hash them itself, which costs a few hundred KB; the lead can
decide whether that is worth it before C5 is ticked.

What I could not test: I have no way to make a build host-dependent outside the
source (for example, a toolchain that embeds its own path), so I cannot show
that the gate catches that class. I tested differences injected into the
artifacts, which is the closest available. I also did not run repro.yml locally;
there is no QEMU-free way to reproduce a windows-latest build on this PC, and
every observation above is from GitHub's runners.

================================================================================
VERDICT: PASS
================================================================================

All three claims in A1.8's message are true as written: different hosts (ubuntu-24.04
and windows-2025-vs2026), the full image and the kernel ELF hashed, and the plant
run failed for the planted reason with no other failure. Both of my own plants
were CAUGHT. The gate still reproduces at today's main. Findings N1 to N3 are
hardening, not false claims.
